            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "It does look counterintuitive, but I couldn't figure out what specific problem does it resolve.\n\nIf this enhancement is needed, we'd better think up a better solution. The one in Vim looks at the first 100 lines and treats a line as cpp if it *starts* with `class`, `namespace`, `template` or `using`. It is not entirely perfect -- it doesn't skip comments and ignores some cpp features, like `std::` and `::`. \n\nAn example of c header that still don't get `c`:\n```c\n// a.h -- valid C, `using` is not a C keyword\n\nstatic inline bool is_using(void) {\n\tbool using = false;\n\tif (/* xxxx */) {\n\t\tusing = true;  /* line starts with \"using\" -> C++ */\n\t}\n\treturn using;\n}\n```\nThe reverse direction is also not perfect: a cpp header that doesn't happen to start a line with one of those four words within the first 100 lines is now detected as `c`. Looks like a best-effort solution.",
            "created_at": "2026-09-19T13:18:37Z",
